WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
<v speaker 1>thanks for making the time. is the door shut your end?

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.800
<v speaker 2>yeah, i'm in one of the little booths. the ones with no air.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:20.667
<v speaker 1>ha, i know them well. right. so, as i said in my message, this is a confidential conversation. i'll keep notes but they'll stay in the case file.

00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:22.600
<v speaker 2>understood.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.400
<v speaker 1>so. you wanted to raise something about your um, team.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:56.400
<v speaker 2>yes. okay. um. so i'll just say it. one of my team, evander wainscott, came to me last week and said that eamon haverstock has been making comments about her, you know, about her appearance, and messaging her late at night, and it's been going on since the conference in the spring. and she was really upset, like really upset, and she asked me not to do anything, but i don't think i can just not do anything? can i?

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.333
<v speaker 1>no, you did the right thing coming to us.

00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:03.933
<v speaker 2>okay. good. because i haven't slept.

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:08.467
<v speaker 1>i understand. can i ask, where was the conference?

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:18.667
<v speaker 2>it was in harrowfield, at basically, the big hotel by the station. and you know, then there was a dinner in little hatherby afterwards.

00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:21.133
<v speaker 1>and were you there?

00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:29.133
<v speaker 2>i was at the conference, not at the you know, dinner. i left early to get the train back to sheffield.

00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:33.333
<v speaker 1>okay. and has anyone else witnessed anything?

00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:47.667
<v speaker 2>she thinks evander might have seen basically, some of the messages. and, um, i think there may have been a similar complaint about him at his last company, someone mentioned it at the christmas do.

00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:52.267
<v speaker 1>okay. i'll note that um, as hearsay, but thank you.

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:53.800
<v speaker 2>sure.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.133
<v speaker 1>now, how is she? is she at work?

00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:00.200
<v speaker 2>she's been off since monday.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:07.067
<v speaker 1>sorry, i'll rephrase, can i ask if there's any health element we need to be aware of?

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:18.067
<v speaker 2>she's been under the doctor for anxiety for a while, and this has knocked her back quite badly, she said she's had a few panic attacks at her desk.

00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:31.067
<v speaker 1>okay. that's really important for us to know, for the support um, side. we like, can refer her to occupational health, and the employee assistance line is open around the clock.

00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:35.333
<v speaker 2>what's the number? i'd like to give it to her.

00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:43.533
<v speaker 1>it's 571-555-0158. and i'd suggest she basically, emails me directly as well, at rosalind@quenby.example.

00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:45.800
<v speaker 2>okay. thank you.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.800
<v speaker 1>now. a couple of process things. is he still in the team?

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
<v speaker 2>yes. that's um, the awful bit. he sits about two desks away.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:08.333
<v speaker 1>right. we'll look at whether we can move him temporarily, maybe to the fenmoor crossing office, or the hot desks at tannery yard. that won't be framed as a punishment.

00:03:08.533 --> 00:03:09.867
<v speaker 2>okay.

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:19.267
<v speaker 1>can i ask, sorry, this is a bit indirect, but is there anything about his performance or his contract we should know about?

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:25.933
<v speaker 2>well. he's on a performance plan already, actually. his numbers have been about...

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:27.533
<v speaker 1>go on.

00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:42.067
<v speaker 2>his numbers have been about forty percent of target for two quarters. and the team's attrition, i mean, honestly, we've lost a third of the team this year and i'm now wondering if this is part of why.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:54.600
<v speaker 1>mm. that's a fair question. for what it's worth, exit interviews flag a manager issue in maybe one in five sort of, cases across the whole business, so it's worth asking.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.267
<v speaker 2>and i know the like, business is, um, i know there's a restructure coming in q1...

00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:04.067
<v speaker 1>i can't comment on that.

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:06.467
<v speaker 2>no, i know. sorry.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:14.467
<v speaker 1>but, for what it's worth, any outcome here is separate from that. i want to you know, be really clear.

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:16.333
<v speaker 2>okay. yes.

00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:27.200
<v speaker 1>so, next steps. i'll open a case, the reference will be 876617. i'll speak to her first, with her consent. then we'll decide on an investigation.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.333
<v speaker 2>will he know it came from me?

00:04:30.533 --> 00:04:36.600
<v speaker 1>we'll do our best. i can't promise anonymity entirely, but we'll be careful.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.333
<v speaker 2>okay. thank you. honestly. this is i mean, the worst bit of the job.

00:04:42.533 --> 00:04:47.333
<v speaker 1>it basically, really is. you're doing fine. speak friday?

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:49.867
<v speaker 2>friday. yes. thanks.
